---
import Mark from "@guiferviz/cheese-bytes-video/astro/Mark.astro";
import StorySection from "@guiferviz/cheese-bytes-video/astro/StorySection.astro";
import VideoVisual from "@guiferviz/cheese-bytes-video/astro/VideoVisual.astro";
import {
  MajorityVideoVisual,
  majorityVideoDefinition,
} from "../components/cave/majority-vote/video/MajorityVideoVisual";
---

<StorySection id="stream" timeline={(t) => {
    t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />Imagine a billion shapes passing right in front of you.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="shapes" />Squares, circles, triangles, diamonds.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="majority" />You're told that one of them appears more than half the time.
      That's a majority, and your job is to find it.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "stream", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="counting" timeline={(t) => {
    t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />The obvious solution is to count.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="count" />Every time a square arrives, we increase its counter. Every circle,
      another counter. Every triangle, another.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="simple" />That works fine with four different shapes.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "counting", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="memory" timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.setSeed, { at: "start", params: { seed: 42 } });
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />But what if there are millions of different shapes?
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="values" />Every new shape needs another counter.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="many" />Eventually, we run out of space, even though the shapes keep arriving.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="question" />So, can we find the majority without keeping a count for every
      different shape?
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "memory", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="cancel" timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "pair-one", duration: 1.65, ease: "linear", params: { value: 1 } });
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "pair-two", duration: 1.65, ease: "linear", params: { value: 2 } });
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "repeat", duration: 3, ease: "linear", params: { value: 4 } });
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />Let's try a small example. We have ten shapes:
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="six" />six squares and four others.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="game" />Pick any two different shapes and remove them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="pair-one" />A square and a circle, for example.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="pair-two" />Then a square and a triangle.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="repeat" />Keep doing that until there are no different pairs left.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="left" />We're left with squares.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "cancel", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="pair-orders" timeline={(t) => {
    t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />But we don't have to include a square in every pair.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="other" />We could start by removing a circle and a triangle.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="any" />That works too. Any two different shapes can cancel.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="repeat" />If we finish this example with these different pairings, we get four
      squares instead of two.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="result" />The number left over can change, but we still finish with squares.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "pair-orders", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="invariant" timeline={(t) => {
    t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />And there's a reason for that. We started with six squares and only
      four other shapes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="why" />To remove a square, we also have to remove something different. There
      aren't enough other shapes to cancel all six.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="trick" />So a shape that makes up more than half of the original collection
      cannot be completely cancelled out.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "invariant", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="online" timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "first", duration: 1.1, ease: "linear", params: { value: 1 } });
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />Now, how do we do this when the shapes arrive one at a time?
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="memory" />Keeping all of them around to pair later would bring us back to the
      memory problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="two" />Instead, we remember a candidate and its strength.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="first" />The first shape becomes our candidate, with a strength of one.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "online", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="algorithm" timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "match", duration: 1.1, ease: "linear", params: { value: 2 } });
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "cancel", duration: 1.1, ease: "linear", params: { value: 3 } });
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "zero", duration: 1.1, ease: "linear", params: { value: 4 } });
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "new", duration: 1.1, ease: "linear", params: { value: 5 } });
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "all", to: "guarantee", ease: "linear", params: { value: 10 } });
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />When the next shape arrives, we compare them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="match" />If they match, we increase the strength. If they're different, we
      decrease it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="cancel" />That decrease is the cancellation: one copy of the candidate cancels
      with the incoming shape.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="zero" />When the strength reaches zero, that whole batch has cancelled out.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="new" />The next shape can become our new candidate, starting again at one.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="all" />We keep doing this, matching or cancelling each incoming shape, until
      the stream ends.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="guarantee" />If a majority exists, our final candidate is guaranteed to be
      that majority.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "algorithm", progress: 1 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="complexity" timeline={(t) => {
    t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />This is the Boyer-Moore Majority Vote Algorithm.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="cost" />We process each element once,
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="two" />using just one candidate and one counter.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "complexity", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="strength" timeline={(t) => {
    t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />But there's something important about that counter.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="balance" />It represents the strength remaining after cancellations,
      <Mark id="actual" />not the candidate's actual frequency.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="compare" />Remember how different pairings left us with different numbers of
      squares? It's the same idea.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "strength", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="catch" timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "candidate", to: "survive", ease: "linear", params: { value: 7 } });
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />And remember our starting condition: we were told that a majority
      existed. What happens if we don't know that?
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="candidate" />The algorithm can still leave us with a candidate.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="survive" />Surviving the cancellations doesn't prove that it appeared more
      than half the time.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="only" />It only means that the candidate survived those cancellations.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "catch", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="verify" timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "scan", to: "none", ease: "linear", params: { value: 0.99 } });
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "none", duration: 0.3, ease: "linear", params: { value: 1 } });
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "yes", to: "confirmed", ease: "linear", params: { value: 2 } });
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />In general, we need a second pass to check.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="scan" />This time, we count only the candidate and compare its frequency with
      half the total.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="none" />If it doesn't exceed that threshold, there isn't a majority.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="yes" />If it does, we have a <Mark id="confirmed" />majority.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "verify", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="friend" timeline={(t) => {
    t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />Now, suppose a friend wants to help.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="split" />We split the stream between us and process our parts at the same
      time.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="lists" />If we each keep a list of counters, we still have to send those lists
      and combine the counts for every different shape.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="summaries" />With cancellations, each of us sends just a candidate and its
      remaining strength.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "friend", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="merge" timeline={(t) => {
    t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />I've got a square with strength two. You've got a circle with
      strength one.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="different" />Our candidates are different, so we cancel one against the other.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="cancel" />One pair cancels, leaving a square with strength one.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="same" />And what if we'd both ended up with the same shape?
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="add" />Then we'd simply add our strengths.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="sum" />Two plus one gives us three.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="guarantee" />And if the full stream has a majority, these cancellations still
      preserve it.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "merge", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="distributed" timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, { from: "combine", duration: 1, ease: "linear", params: { value: 1 } });
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />This is useful when large datasets are spread across machines in a
      cluster.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="three" />Each machine processes its own part,
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="summaries" />and we <Mark id="combine" />combine these small summaries.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="verify" />The verification can be distributed too: <Mark id="counts" />each machine counts the
      final candidate in its part, <Mark id="total" />and we add the results.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "distributed", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="extension" timeline={(t) => {
    t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />There's also an extension called Misra-Gries that keeps several
      candidates and gives frequency estimates with a bounded error.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="later" />But we'll leave that for another video.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "extension", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection id="closing" holdAfter={2} timeline={(t) => {
    t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
  }}>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />We started with a billion shapes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="remember" />All we needed to remember was <Mark id="candidate" />one candidate and <Mark id="counter" />one counter.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="survive" />Because a true majority can't be completely cancelled out.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual initial={{ scene: "closing", progress: 0 }} client:only="react" />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<style is:global>
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Majority Iosevka";
    src: url("/fonts/IosevkaTermSlabNerdFontPropo-Regular.woff2")
      format("woff2");
    font-weight: 400;
    font-display: block;
  }
  [data-majority-scene] {
    font-family: "Majority Iosevka", monospace !important;
  }
</style>
